@anon.3c5d4856225436a016f648d8ca8332b3.2820 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ [2452 x i8] }> <{ [2452 x i8] c"# `negative_impls`\0A\0AThe tracking issue for this feature is [#68318].\0A\0A[#68318]: https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/68318\0A\0A----\0A\0AWith the feature gate `negative_impls`, you can write negative impls as well as positive ones:\0A\0A```rust\0A#![feature(negative_impls)]\0Atrait DerefMut { }\0Aimpl<T: ?Sized> !DerefMut for &T { }\0A```\0A\0ANegative impls indicate a semver guarantee that the given trait will not be implemented for the given types. Negative impls play an additional purpose for auto traits, described below.\0A\0ANegative impls have the following characteristics:\0A\0A* They do not have any items.\0A* They must obey the orphan rules as if they were a positive impl.\0A* They cannot \22overlap\22 with any positive impls.\0A\0A## Semver interaction\0A\0AIt is a breaking change to remove a negative impl. Negative impls are a commitment not to implement the given trait for the named types.\0A\0A## Orphan and overlap rules\0A\0ANegative impls must obey the same orphan rules as a positive impl. This implies you cannot add a negative impl for types defined in upstream crates and so forth.\0A\0ASimilarly, negative impls cannot overlap with positive impls, again using the same \22overlap\22 check that we ordinarily use to determine if two impls overlap. (Note that positive impls typically cannot overlap with one another either, except as permitted by specialization.)\0A\0A## Interaction with auto traits\0A\0ADeclaring a negative impl `impl !SomeAutoTrait for SomeType` for an\0Aauto-trait serves two purposes:\0A\0A* as with any trait, it declares that `SomeType` will never implement `SomeAutoTrait`;\0A* it disables the automatic `SomeType: SomeAutoTrait` impl that would otherwise have been generated.\0A\0ANote that, at present, there is no way to indicate that a given type\0Adoes not implement an auto trait *but that it may do so in the\0Afuture*. For ordinary types, this is done by simply not declaring any\0Aimpl at all, but that is not an option for auto traits. A workaround\0Ais that one could embed a marker type as one of the fields, where the\0Amarker type is `!AutoTrait`.\0A\0A## Immediate uses\0A\0ANegative impls are used to declare that `&T: !DerefMut`  and `&mut T: !Clone`, as required to fix the soundness of `Pin` described in [#66544](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/66544).\0A\0AThis serves two purposes:\0A\0A* For proving the correctness of unsafe code, we can use that impl as evidence that no `DerefMut` or `Clone` impl exists.\0A* It prevents downstream crates from creating such impls.\0A" }>, align 1
