@anon.3c5d4856225436a016f648d8ca8332b3.3144 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ [4551 x i8] }> <{ [4551 x i8] c"# `test`\0A\0AThe tracking issue for this feature is: None.\0A\0A------------------------\0A\0AThe internals of the `test` crate are unstable, behind the `test` flag.  The\0Amost widely used part of the `test` crate are benchmark tests, which can test\0Athe performance of your code.  Let's make our `src/lib.rs` look like this\0A(comments elided):\0A\0A```rust,no_run\0A#![feature(test)]\0A\0Aextern crate test;\0A\0Apub fn add_two(a: i32) -> i32 {\0A    a + 2\0A}\0A\0A#[cfg(test)]\0Amod tests {\0A    use super::*;\0A    use test::Bencher;\0A\0A    #[test]\0A    fn it_works() {\0A        assert_eq!(4, add_two(2));\0A    }\0A\0A    #[bench]\0A    fn bench_add_two(b: &mut Bencher) {\0A        b.iter(|| add_two(2));\0A    }\0A}\0A```\0A\0ANote the `test` feature gate, which enables this unstable feature.\0A\0AWe've imported the `test` crate, which contains our benchmarking support.\0AWe have a new function as well, with the `bench` attribute. Unlike regular\0Atests, which take no arguments, benchmark tests take a `&mut Bencher`. This\0A`Bencher` provides an `iter` method, which takes a closure. This closure\0Acontains the code we'd like to benchmark.\0A\0AWe can run benchmark tests with `cargo bench`:\0A\0A```bash\0A$ cargo bench\0A   Compiling adder v0.0.1 (file:///home/steve/tmp/adder)\0A     Running target/release/adder-91b3e234d4ed382a\0A\0Arunning 2 tests\0Atest tests::it_works ... ignored\0Atest tests::bench_add_two ... bench:         1 ns/iter (+/- 0)\0A\0Atest result: ok. 0 passed; 0 failed; 1 ignored; 1 measured\0A```\0A\0AOur non-benchmark test was ignored. You may have noticed that `cargo bench`\0Atakes a bit longer than `cargo test`. This is because Rust runs our benchmark\0Aa number of times, and then takes the average. Because we're doing so little\0Awork in this example, we have a `1 ns/iter (+/- 0)`, but this would show\0Athe variance if there was one.\0A\0AAdvice on writing benchmarks:\0A\0A\0A* Move setup code outside the `iter` loop; only put the part you want to measure inside\0A* Make the code do \22the same thing\22 on each iteration; do not accumulate or change state\0A* Make the outer function idempotent too; the benchmark runner is likely to run\0A  it many times\0A*  Make the inner `iter` loop short and fast so benchmark runs are fast and the\0A   calibrator can adjust the run-length at fine resolution\0A* Make the code in the `iter` loop do something simple, to assist in pinpointing\0A  performance improvements (or regressions)\0A\0A## Gotcha: optimizations\0A\0AThere's another tricky part to writing benchmarks: benchmarks compiled with\0Aoptimizations activated can be dramatically changed by the optimizer so that\0Athe benchmark is no longer benchmarking what one expects. For example, the\0Acompiler might recognize that some calculation has no external effects and\0Aremove it entirely.\0A\0A```rust,no_run\0A#![feature(test)]\0A\0Aextern crate test;\0Ause test::Bencher;\0A\0A#[bench]\0Afn bench_xor_1000_ints(b: &mut Bencher) {\0A    b.iter(|| {\0A        (0..1000).fold(0, |old, new| old ^ new);\0A    });\0A}\0A```\0A\0Agives the following results\0A\0A```text\0Arunning 1 test\0Atest bench_xor_1000_ints ... bench:         0 ns/iter (+/- 0)\0A\0Atest result: ok. 0 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 1 measured\0A```\0A\0AThe benchmarking runner offers two ways to avoid this. Either, the closure that\0Athe `iter` method receives can return an arbitrary value which forces the\0Aoptimizer to consider the result used and ensures it cannot remove the\0Acomputation entirely. This could be done for the example above by adjusting the\0A`b.iter` call to\0A\0A```rust\0A# struct X;\0A# impl X { fn iter<T, F>(&self, _: F) where F: FnMut() -> T {} } let b = X;\0Ab.iter(|| {\0A    // Note lack of `;` (could also use an explicit `return`).\0A    (0..1000).fold(0, |old, new| old ^ new)\0A});\0A```\0A\0AOr, the other option is to call the generic `test::black_box` function, which\0Ais an opaque \22black box\22 to the optimizer and so forces it to consider any\0Aargument as used.\0A\0A```rust\0A#![feature(test)]\0A\0Aextern crate test;\0A\0A# fn main() {\0A# struct X;\0A# impl X { fn iter<T, F>(&self, _: F) where F: FnMut() -> T {} } let b = X;\0Ab.iter(|| {\0A    let n = test::black_box(1000);\0A\0A    (0..n).fold(0, |a, b| a ^ b)\0A})\0A# }\0A```\0A\0ANeither of these read or modify the value, and are very cheap for small values.\0ALarger values can be passed indirectly to reduce overhead (e.g.\0A`black_box(&huge_struct)`).\0A\0APerforming either of the above changes gives the following benchmarking results\0A\0A```text\0Arunning 1 test\0Atest bench_xor_1000_ints ... bench:       131 ns/iter (+/- 3)\0A\0Atest result: ok. 0 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 1 measured\0A```\0A\0AHowever, the optimizer can still modify a testcase in an undesirable manner\0Aeven when using either of the above.\0A" }>, align 1
